\ Bounded-memory regression for scope-exit reaping of tracked spawn tasks.
\
\ Repeatedly enter a short-lived nested task-scope that spawns tracked
\ children and exits. Each scope-exit now reaps its completed tracked
\ children, so their task structs, contexts, and arenas are freed and
\ steady-state memory stays bounded across the loop. A nested task-scope
\ blocks until every child is terminal, so no explicit yield is needed for
\ the children to finish before the scope drains and reaps them.
\
\ Before scope-exit reaping, every completed tracked child was retained in
\ the scheduler's finished_tasks until scheduler teardown. This loop reaps
\ roughly 24000 tasks (8000 scopes, three tracked tasks each: the scope
\ coordinator plus two spawned children), so a regression back to that
\ behavior retains all of them and exhausts the 8 MB cap set in the `.flags`
\ sidecar partway through, dying with a memory-limit error instead of
\ printing the marker.
\
\ The `.flags` sidecar pins `--threads=1`. Same-worker scope-exit reaping only
\ reaps children whose home worker is the exiting worker, so every child must
\ be local to the reaping worker for this to stay bounded. Cross-worker
\ reaping is a follow-up, so the pin is required even under the threads=auto
\ test variant.

[
  8000 [
    [
      [ 1 1 + drop ] spawn drop
      [ 2 2 + drop ] spawn drop
    ] task-scope
  ] times
] task-scope

"bounded" print-line
